 being forced to adapt like  the mineral extraction industry which  accounts for about 1.3 billion dollars  in annual economic output according to a  report commissioned by the great salt  lake advisory council magnesium  potassium and lithium are found in  world-class concentrations in the great  salt lake joe havasi is the director of  natural resources for compass minerals  one of the largest mineral extraction  companies on the lake  it produces key ingredients used in  lithium-ion batteries for electric  vehicles  it is also north america's largest  producer of potassium sulfate a  fertilizer required to grow high-end  crops like fruits and nuts what we do is  we draw brine in from the great salt  lake into large open evaporation ponds  in the evaporation ponds the water  evaporates leaving beds of crystalline  recoverable minerals  this process contributes to lower lake  levels too accounting for about 13 of  total water diversion the lake is also a  critical resource for the manufacturer  us magnesium a company that is the sole  provider of primary magnesium metal in  the country  so if you hold a soda pop can in your  hand or if you go sit on a stadium  bleacher that is made of aluminum the  magnesium in those cans and in those  stadium bleachers comes from the great  salt lake  lower lake levels have already forced us  magnesium to move a pump station to get  water to its evaporation ponds  other companies around the lake have had  to expand their canal systems which take  complicated permits and cost millions of  dollars a dwindling lake would also  reduce available jobs so broadly the  mineral extraction industry on the great  salt lake employs over 5000 people so  there would be you know hundreds if not  thousands of of job losses associated  with that the drying lake is also  affecting an industry that is crucial to  the local economy and even your own  seafood consumption  45 of the world's brine shrimp comes  from this lake  and they're used to raise 